routine_steps.claim:
On $Z$, the functional $F(v)=-\int_{\Omega^-}
abla u_0^-\cdot
abla v^-$ is bounded by $\|
abla u_0^-\|_2a_Z(v,v)^{1/2}$. Apply [A2] to obtain a unique $z\in Z$ such that $a_Z(z,v)=F(v)$ for every $v\in Z$. Then $u=u_0+z\in\mathcal A_\phi$ satisfies $\int_{\Omega^-}
abla u^-\cdot
abla v^-+\int_{\Omega^+}
abla u^+\cdot
abla v^+=0$ for every $v\in Z$. For any $v\in Z$, expansion gives $\mathcal E(u+v)=\mathcal E(u)+\mathcal E(v)$, so $u$ is the unique minimizer and $\mathcal E(u)\le\mathcal E(u_0)$. Taking compactly supported tests inside either side proves weak harmonicity.

If $u=D\phi$ and $v=D\psi$, their sum belongs to $\mathcal A_{\phi+\psi}$ and satisfies the stationarity equation because each summand does; uniqueness of the minimizer gives $D(\phi+\psi)=D\phi+D\psi$, and scalar homogeneity follows in the same way. The interior weak Green formula and the exterior finite-energy Green identity justified in the weak layer definition give $\int_{\Omega^-}
abla u^-\cdot
abla v^-+\int_{\Omega^+}
abla u^+\cdot
abla v^+=\langle\partial_n^-u,\gamma^-vangle-\langle\partial_n^+u,\gamma^+vangle.$ Since $u$ has common conormal $q_\phi$ and $\gamma^-v-\gamma^+v=-\psi$, the right side is $-\langle q_\phi,\psiangle=\langle W\phi,\psiangle$. The left side is symmetric in $u,v$, so $W$ is symmetric. Setting $\psi=\phi$ gives $\langle W\phi,\phiangle=\mathcal E(D\phi)\ge0$.

Each layer solves $P_ku=0$ on either side by its source identity. For any such interior restriction define $\langle q^-,gangle=\int_{\Omega^-}(
abla u\cdot\overline{
abla Lg}-k^2u\overline{Lg})$. Differences of lifts are in $H^1_0$, so the weak PDE proves independence; step 1.1 gives $\|q^-\|_{H^{-1/2}}\le C\|u\|_{H^1}$. For the exterior choose a compactly supported lift in a bounded collar and use minus this integral, because the exterior's outward normal on $\Gamma$ is $-n$. This gives $q^+$ with the same bound. They give Green's identity for every interior $H^1$ test and every exterior $H^1$ test supported in a bounded collar and vanishing on its artificial outer boundary. More generally, on a bounded truncation $U=B_R\setminus\overline{\Omega^-}$ with the sphere separated from $\Gamma$, subtract such a compact inner trace lift from a test $v\in H^1(U)$. The remaining test has zero trace on $\Gamma$, and integration by parts near the smooth outer sphere, together with the weak PDE for zero-trace tests, gives $\int_U(
abla u\cdot\overline{
abla v}-k^2u ar v)=\int_{\partial B_R}\partial_ru ar v\,dS-\langle q^+,\gamma^+vangle$ whenever $u$ is smooth near that sphere. Approximation gives the same identity with a weak outer conormal if necessary. No boundary term at infinity is discarded. All these identities use the declared sesquilinear convention.
